# CODEOWNERS — trust-critical paths for credential-badges.
# Review: auto-request only
#
# DOCUMENT-ONLY gate (v1). This file records who must review changes to the
# paths below. It is intentionally NOT yet backed by branch-protection
# required-reviewers — a named team with >=2 required reviewers is deferred to
# productionization (deployment plan P1bis-09; "v1 has James as owner"). The
# cheap companion control is disabling force-push to the default branch.
#
# Scoped to Rungs 3-4 (did:web resolves; issuer Profile = AttestationHost),
# extended at Rung 8.3 with the served status-list JSON (per the plan's
# "later rungs extend this to the status-list JSON") and at Rung 8.6 with the
# kill-switch flip tool + runbook.
#
# A-022 R7 (a named code owner on every public repo) adds the default owner
# line below and a second named reviewer on every trust-critical path.

# Default owner for every path. GitHub applies the last matching pattern, so
# the trust-critical entries below still win for their paths.
*                              @workshop-maybe

# The did:web DID document and its deterministic regenerator — this publishes
# the issuer signing key. A wrong or drifted key here silently breaks OB 3.0
# verification for every credential, so changes get a deliberate review. The
# whole /.well-known/** is gated (not just did.json) so any future sibling
# served on this forever-public path is reviewed too. did-pin.test.ts holds the
# pinned key constant (the actual invariant), so it is gated alongside.
/.well-known/**                @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/tools/gen-did-json.ts         @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/tools/did-pin.test.ts         @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot

# The hosted OB 3.0 issuer Profile and the served JSON-LD contexts (Rung 4;
# v1 added by the context-version bump). The Profile declares the issuer as
# ["Profile","AttestationHost"] and names the did:web subject; the context
# DEFINES the AttestationHost extension term the Profile references. A wrong
# id, a dropped type, or an undefined term silently breaks strict-verifier
# expansion for every credential, so changes get a deliberate review.
# Published context versions are immutable forever — the sha256 pins live in
# tools/context-freeze.test.ts, which is gated alongside (same pattern as
# did-pin.test.ts). The whole /context/** is gated (matching /.well-known/**
# and /status/**) so any future version or stray sibling on this forever-
# public path is reviewed too. tools/issuer-profile.test.ts pins the profile
# shape invariant, so it is gated alongside as well.
/issuer/profile.jsonld         @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/context/**                    @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/tools/context-freeze.test.ts  @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/tools/issuer-profile.test.ts  @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot

# The served key-epoch BitstringStatusList credential and its builder/tests
# (Rung 8.3). A wrong bit here flags every credential signed under a key
# version as suspended in third-party verifier UIs; a wrong encodedList
# silently breaks status checks — changes get a deliberate review. The whole
# /status/** is gated so any future sibling on this forever-public path is
# reviewed too.
/status/**                             @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/signing/status-list.ts        @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/signing/status-list.test.ts @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot

# The signing surface the retire-spike move concentrated under signing/
# (credential-badges#124). sign.ts and sign-class.ts are the KMS entry points
# (what gets signed, under what gate); sign-status-list.ts writes the served
# kill-switch artifact; class-artifacts/** are the signed credentials every
# served badge is baked from. expansion-pin.dep-test.ts holds the canonical-
# dataset pins, so it is gated alongside (same pattern as did-pin.test.ts).
# bake-class.ts is left out on purpose: it writes badges/, which is
# presentation-layer and not gated.
/signing/sign.ts                     @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/signing/sign-class.ts               @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/signing/sign-status-list.ts         @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/signing/class-artifacts/**          @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/signing/expansion-pin.dep-test.ts   @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot

# The key-compromise kill-switch (Rung 8.6; deployment plan Decision 3 +
# CODEOWNERS glob set). The flip tool prepares the suspension flip for a whole
# key epoch — a wrong flip suspends every credential signed under a key
# version, including the flagship badge — and the runbook is the procedure the
# flip PR follows; both get a deliberate review.
/tools/flip-status-bit.ts        @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/tools/flip-status-bit.test.ts   @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/docs/runbooks/key-compromise.md @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot

# Served-file allowlist controls. Adding a public, forever-served path must be
# a deliberate reviewed act — these four files are the allowlist surface
# (Dockerfile COPY, build-context re-includes, nginx location, CI gate).
/Dockerfile                    @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/.dockerignore                 @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/nginx/default.conf.template   @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/scripts/ci/check-allowlist.sh @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot

# Alias safety gate (plan KTD-5). Aliases are untrusted on-chain data written
# into SVG, a format browsers execute — the live holder set contains an XSS
# payload. This allowlist is the only control between that input and a
# generated artifact, so widening its charset gets a deliberate review.
/scripts/alias_gate.py            @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
/scripts/tests/test_alias_gate.py @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot

# The liveness guard for this file. GitHub never warns about a pattern that
# matches nothing, so a path move can silently retire any gate above — the
# retire-spike move (2026-08) relocated the two status-list entries and only
# a hand check caught it. codeowners.test.ts turns that into a red build.
# Gated alongside the thing it protects, same as did-pin.test.ts and
# context-freeze.test.ts: deleting the guard should be a deliberate act.
/tools/codeowners.test.ts      @workshop-maybe @adrian1-dot
